## The Harvesters: a day's work
sources:
    - id: met
      title: The Harvesters (19.164)
      authors: The Metropolitan Museum of Art
      year: 2026
      finding: Bruegel, 1565, oil on wood, 119 × 162 cm; Rogers Fund, 1919
      url: https://collectionapi.metmuseum.org/public/collection/v1/objects/435809
    - id: wiki
      title: The Harvesters (painting)
      authors: Wikipedia
      year: 2026
      finding: Commissioned by Niclaes Jongelinck for a cycle of the seasons, five panels surviving; peasants eat while others harvest; pear picking; bathers; cock throwing on the green
      url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Harvesters_(painting)
    - id: davis
      title: 'Labors of the Month, August: Pieter Bruegel'
      authors: Davis Art, Curator's Corner
      year: 2024
      finding: Six panels for Jongelinck's country house near Antwerp, probably hung in its dining room; the labours-of-the-months tradition
      url: https://www.davisart.com/blogs/curators-corner/labors-of-the-month-august-pieter-bruegel-ca-1525-1569-flanders/
    - id: agd
      title: The Harvesters by Pieter Bruegel (In the Picture 227)
      authors: Andrew Graham-Dixon
      year: 2003
      finding: One of the series for Jongelinck; five survive, this the only one in America
      url: https://andrewgrahamdixon.com/archive/itp-227-the-harvesters-by-pieter-bruegel.html
    - id: trh
      title: Très Riches Heures du Duc de Berry, July
      authors: Limbourg brothers
      year: 1416
      finding: 'Calendar page for July: the harvest and sheep shearing below the duke''s castle'

    card title "The Harvesters"

What does a day's work look like?
    image art cites ["met","wiki"] credit "Bruegel, 1565. The Met, New York. Public domain." x 500 y 335 width 900 url https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/05/Pieter_Bruegel_the_Elder-_The_Harvesters_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg/1920px-Pieter_Bruegel_the_Elder-_The_Harvesters_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg look screen regions.bathers [0.2,0.48,0.07,0.04] regions.binders [0.75,0.57,0.08,0.1] regions.bread [0.66,0.8,0.055,0.12] regions.cart [0.065,0.49,0.07,0.05] regions.corner [0.62,0.62,0.38,0.38] regions.corner_bl [0,0.7,0.13,0.3] regions.drinker [0.685,0.7,0.06,0.06] regions.far_mower [0.655,0.525,0.05,0.055] regions.game [0.355,0.465,0.09,0.045] regions.gatherers [0.92,0.5,0.08,0.06] regions.jug_carrier [0.315,0.645,0.05,0.13] regions.jug_cool [0.08,0.875,0.045,0.11] regions.meal [0.65,0.7,0.28,0.28] regions.mowers [0.145,0.68,0.12,0.28] regions.pear_tree [0.86,0.28,0.14,0.3] regions.pears_cloth [0.8,0.84,0.04,0.09] regions.picker [0.95,0.31,0.03,0.05] regions.ships [0.34,0.32,0.14,0.05] regions.shocks [0.83,0.54,0.17,0.2] regions.sky [0,0,0.45,0.3] regions.sleeper [0.445,0.775,0.19,0.195] regions.village [0.03,0.3,0.5,0.32] regions.wheat [0.1,0.42,0.6,0.3] regions.workers [0.14,0.55,0.86,0.45]

Bruegel answered with a wheat field at the height of summer, in 1565.
    highlight art:wheat strength soft

In the Fall of Icarus, a myth still hid in the corner. Here it's gone altogether: just a harvest, from the front edge to the horizon.
    highlight art:corner_bl art:mowers art:meal art:village art:ships strength soft

    camera on ["art@[0.04,0.62,0.3,0.38]"]

On the left, two men are still mowing, swinging long scythes into the standing wheat.
    highlight art:mowers

Their drink waits for them: a jug tucked into the uncut stalks.
    highlight art:jug_cool

    camera reset true

Down a path cut through the grain comes another man, a jug in each hand.
    highlight art:jug_carrier

    camera center.ref art@[0.77,0.575,0.04,0.04] zoom 4

Behind the tree, women bend to bind the cut wheat into sheave (@highlight art:binders art:shocks@)s, and the sheaves are stood up in stacks to dry.

    camera reset true

    ask "Find the man who has stopped completely. Tap him." spot sleeper on art
        intro: But not everyone is working.
        right: There he is, flat out under the tree.
        wrong: Look at the foot of the big tree, in the shade.

    camera on ["art@[0.42,0.6,0.3,0.4]"]

He lies in the shade with his shirt open and his legs flung wide, too tired even to eat.
    highlight art:sleeper strength strong rest blur lift true

    camera on ["art:meal"]

Beside him, the rest have stopped for a meal, most likely the midday break.
    highlight art:meal lift true

One cuts bread from a basket, one tips back a jug, others spoon from bowls, and there are pears on the cloth.
    highlight art:bread art:drinker art:pears_cloth

    camera on ["art@[0.76,0.26,0.24,0.32]"]

The pears come from the tree on the right, where someone up in the branches is picking and others gather from the ground.
    highlight art:picker art:gatherers

    camera on ["art@[0.19,0.44,0.27,0.08]"]

Further off, the village keeps its own hours: people bathe in a pond, and on the green others play cock throwing, a cruel old game of hurling sticks at a tethered rooster.
    highlight art:bathers art:game

    camera reset true

So why make peasants the whole subject?
    highlight art:meal art:sleeper strength soft

The answer starts in a calendar.
    image july cites ["trh"] credit "Très Riches Heures, c. 1415" x 215 y 420 width 330 url https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/be/Les_Tr%C3%A8s_Riches_Heures_du_duc_de_Berry_juillet.jpg/960px-Les_Tr%C3%A8s_Riches_Heures_du_duc_de_Berry_juillet.jpg look screen view [0,0.4,1,0.6] regions.castle [0.03,0.33,0.94,0.26] regions.reapers [0.12,0.57,0.6,0.22] reveal develop

Prayer books had  (@highlight july:castle july:reapers@)calendar pages for each month's labour.

Here is July in a French duke's book, made around 1415: two small reapers under his castle.
    highlight july:reapers

Bruegel took that little scene, brought the workers to the front, and made it a panel more than one and a half metres wide.
    highlight art:mowers art:meal

    erase july

It was one of a set of six seasons, painted for Niclaes Jongelinck, a rich Antwerp merchant. Five survive, and this is the only one in America.
    highlight art:mowers art:meal strength soft

    camera on ["art:meal"]

The set probably hung in his country house, perhaps in the dining room: a merchant at dinner, looking at peasants at lunch.
    highlight art:meal rest paper lift true

    camera reset true

No saint, no hero, no myth. Th (@highlight art:workers rest grey lift true@)e year itself is the story, and the people who grow the bread carry it.

Bruegel paints them tired, hungry, a little comic, and still worth a big panel of their own.
    highlight art:sleeper art:meal strength soft

    quiz "A photographer wants to show a day in a hospital the way Bruegel shows the harvest. Which plan follows his method?"
        right: The one wide shot: ordinary staff working, eating and resting side by side, with nobody singled out as the star.
        wrong: Bruegel puts no single star in front, and he fits work, food and rest into one picture.
        * Twelve monthly shots, one worker each, like a calendar
        * A grand shot of the surgeon at the moment of a cure
        * A portrait of the hospital's donor at lunch
        + One wide shot of staff working, eating, resting

Next time you take a lunch break with the people you work with, look around: that's a Bruegel.
    highlight art:meal lift true

